---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/*
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Written from what the code actually does, not from a template: every claim here is
 * checkable against the repo, and several are enforced by tests —
 * `apps/web/e2e/key-never-leaks.spec.ts` proves the decryption key never reaches the
 * network, and `test/sw-policy.test.ts` proves the service worker refuses to cache
 * ciphertext or decrypted images.
 *
 * If the behaviour changes, this page has to change with it. It is a promise, not marketing.
 */

const updated = "21 October 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — Hopshot"
  description="What Hopshot stores, what it doesn't, and where things go."
>
  <main class="mx-auto flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-8 px-6 py-16">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <h1 class="text-display">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="text-small text-text-3">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">The short version</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        Your screenshots stay on your computer. When you share one or send it to your phone,
        it is encrypted on your device first, and the key never leaves it — so we hold bytes
        we cannot read. Everything expires on its own.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">What stays local</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        Captures, their thumbnails and the shelf database live in Hopshot's own folder in your
        user profile. Nothing is uploaded unless you share a screenshot or turn on phone sync.
        Deleting an item deletes the file.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">What we store when you share</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        Sharing a screenshot or syncing it to your phone uploads it <em>already encrypted</em>
        (AES-256-GCM). On our servers we hold:
      </p>
      <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-body text-text-2" style="list-style: disc">
        <li>the encrypted image, which we cannot decrypt</li>
        <li>its size, creation time and expiry time</li>
        <li>a random id, generated on your device</li>
        <li>hashes of the access tokens — never the tokens themselves</li>
        <li>
          a random install identifier, used only to count your own daily sends against your
          own limit. It is not linked to you or to any account.
        </li>
        <li>
          a one-way hash of your IP address, salted with the date, kept for 7 days to stop
          abuse. We do not store raw IP addresses.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        The file name, dimensions and device name are encrypted too, so even those are not
        visible to us.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">Why we can't read your screenshots</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        The decryption key is created on your device and travels only in the part of a link
        after the <code style="font-family: var(--mono)">#</code>. Browsers never send that
        part to a server — it is a deliberate property of the web, not a policy we apply. So a
        share link works for whoever has the link, and does not work for us.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        The same is true of phone sync: your computer and your phones share a key that we
        never receive.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">Expiry</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        Share links expire after 24 hours by default, and up to 7 days with Sync. Shelf items
        expire after the period you choose, 24 hours by default. Expired data is deleted
        along with the encrypted file. You can also delete a link at any time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">Analytics</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        Anonymous usage statistics and crash reports are <strong>off</strong> unless you turn
        them on. Hopshot does not include third-party trackers, and the app does not phone
        home to tell us what you captured.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">Who else is involved</h2>
      <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-body text-text-2" style="list-style: disc">
        <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the service and stores the encrypted files.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stripe</strong> processes payments. They receive your name, billing address
          and card details; we never see your card. We keep your email, name and Stripe
          customer id so a purchase can be restored.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Resend</strong> delivers receipts and restore codes.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">Deleting your data</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        Settings → Privacy → <strong>Delete all my cloud data</strong> removes your synced
        items, your paired devices and the encrypted files, immediately. Your local shelf is
        untouched. To delete purchase records, email us and we'll remove what we are not
        required to keep for tax purposes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-heading">Contact</h2>
      <p class="text-body text-text-2">
        Questions about any of this: <a style="color: var(--accent-text)" href="mailto:hello@hopshot.xyz">hello@hopshot.xyz</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
